Media queries are used in CSS to allow for responsive design, enabling web pages to adapt to different devices and screen sizes. They consist of a media type and one or more expressions that check against properties of the output device such as width, height, orientation, resolution, and others.

The syntax for a media query is as follows:

```css
@media <media-query> {
  /* CSS rules */
}
```

Where `<media-query>` is an expression like `screen and (max-width: 600px)`.

For example, you might use a media query to apply styles specifically for smaller screens:

```css
@media screen and (max-width: 600px) {
  body {
    font-size: 0.8em;
  }
}
```

In this example, the font size is reduced for screens narrower than 600 pixels. This helps ensure that the website remains readable and usable on mobile devices.

